RayCAD patch to QuickJS-NG 0.18.0 (MIT; this file is under the same terms).

What: the engine calls the host's interrupt handler (JS_SetInterruptHandler) every 100 loop back-edges or calls
instead of every 10,000.

Why: RayCAD's post host (src/post/engine.cpp) enforces its CPU-time limit and the user's cancel through that handler.
With the stock value a loop whose time is spent inside native calls (a.sort(); a.reverse() on 20,000 strings) was
stopped 7.4 to 9.2 s after a 0.5 s limit; at 100 it stopped on time, and posting 1,000,000 moves took the same time
(662 to 716 ms against 662 to 720 ms). Measured in spike S8 (spikes/s8-quickjs/REPORT.md). The constant has no
#ifndef upstream, so changing it takes a patch.

Upstream: not reported; asking for a setter (JS_SetInterruptCounter or similar) would let this patch go.

diff --git a/quickjs.c b/quickjs.c
--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -555,7 +555,10 @@ enum {

 /* must be large enough to have a negligible runtime cost and small
    enough to call the interrupt callback often. */
-#define JS_INTERRUPT_COUNTER_INIT 10000
+/* RayCAD patch 0001: 100 instead of 10000. The host's CPU limit and cancel
+   act through the interrupt callback; at 10000 a loop over native calls
+   (Array.prototype.sort) was stopped seconds late (spike S8). */
+#define JS_INTERRUPT_COUNTER_INIT 100

 struct JSContext {
     JSGCObjectHeader header; /* must come first */
